## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of attention scaling, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs in production-grade transformers.

## Key Concepts
- Block-local attention and memory scaling
- Attention masking within a window
- Q/K/V reshaping for block processing
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs latency vs memory

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: local attention in blocks
function localAttention(Q, K, V, block=256, window=128){
  // Q,K,V: [B, T, D]
  const B = Q.length, T = Q[0].length, D = Q[0][0].length;
  let O = Array.from({length:B}, () => Array.from({length:T}, () => new Array(D).fill(0)));
  for (let b=0; b<B; b++){
    for (let s=0; s<T; s+=block){
      const end = Math.min(T, s+block);
      for (let t=s; t<end; t++){
        const wStart = Math.max(0, t-window);
        const wEnd = Math.min(T, t+window+1);
        // compute attention over [wStart, wEnd)
        // A = softmax((Q[t]·K[w].T)/sqrt(D))
        // O[b][t] = sum_{w=wStart}^{wEnd-1} A[w]·V[w]
      }
    }
  }
  return O;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does changing window size affect long-range dependency capture and memory?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect degradation when switching from global to local attention?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to architect a scalable cross-lingual RAG pipeline handling chunking, multilingual embeddings, and vector stores with versioning and TTL. It also probes cross-language retrieval strategies and evaluation plans to reduce hallucinations.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual embeddings (LaBSE, mBERT) and language tagging
- Document-type aware chunking strategies
- Vector DB indexing with versioning, TTL/decay, and provenance
- Cross-language retrieval + fallback translation
- Evaluation: latency, recall across languages, hallucination rate

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: bilingual embedder + cross-language retrieve
const embedder = new LaBSEEmbedder({ model: 'labse' })
const index = new VectorDB({ name: 'docs', ... })
async function ingest(doc){
  const chunks = chunkDoc(doc, typeRules[doc.type])
  for (const c of chunks){
    index.upsert({ id: c.id, vec: embedder.embed(c.text), meta: { lang: c.lang, version: doc.version, ts: doc.ts }})
  }
}
async function query(q, lang){
  const v = embedder.embed(q)
  const hits = index.query(v, { k: 5, lang })
  // cross-language re-rank can involve translating top hits or language-agnostic scoring
  return hits
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new languages or drift in embeddings?
- How would you measure cross-language retrieval effectiveness in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical retrieval system design under real constraints: multi-tenant isolation, chunking strategies, vector DB selection, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking strategy: window size, overlap
- Embeddings and vector DB: model choice, dim, indexing
- Retrieval pipeline: dense + lexical fallback, reranking
- Multi-tenant concerns: RBAC, isolation, per-tenant relevance tuning
- Observability: metrics, traces, SLOs
- Update strategy: incremental indexing, versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for two-stage retriever
async function retrieve(query, tenantId){
  const blocks = chunkDocsForTenant(tenantId);
  const qVec = embed(query);
  const topDense = vectorDB.search(qVec, {k: 10, tenant: tenantId});
  const topLexical = lexicalSearch(query, tenantId);
  const merged = rerank([topDense, topLexical], query);
  return topK(merged, 5);
}
```

